 \metareviewcomment{2}
 Moderate the novelty claims and better distinguish MORPH from existing SQL translation approaches, particularly SEDAR, RISE, QTRAN, and CrackSQL. Explain more concretely what AQT and its associated mechanisms contribute beyond existing techniques, especially in the context of DBMS fuzzing.
 
 \reply
-We have revised Sections~I, II, III, and VI to clarify how \tool{} relates to existing whole-test-case transfer and fuzzer-oriented seed adaptation approaches, in particular \sedar{}. We have revised the related work in Section~VI to state that \tool{} adopts related techniques, including structural decomposition, localized translation, and dialect-aware retrieval, but targets fuzzing seed transfer, and to distinguish \tool{} from \sedar{}, CrackSQL, RISE, and QTRAN in terms of goals, challenges, methods, and results.
+We have revised Sections~I (Introduction), II (Background and Motivation), III (Approach), and VI (Related Work) to clarify how \tool{} relates to existing whole-test-case transfer and fuzzer-oriented seed adaptation approaches, in particular \sedar{}. We have revised the related work in Section~VI (Related Work) to state that \tool{} adopts related techniques, including structural decomposition, localized translation, and dialect-aware retrieval, but targets fuzzing seed transfer, and to distinguish \tool{} from \sedar{}, CrackSQL, RISE, and QTRAN in terms of goals, challenges, methods, and results.
 
 \textbf{Different goals.} CrackSQL and RISE target query migration: they translate one self-contained query or procedural artifact at a time and aim to produce an equivalent target query. QTRAN targets oracle transfer: it translates a query and regenerates its target-side mutant so that the metamorphic relation is preserved for logic-bug detection. \sedar{} and \tool{} share the goal of transferring existing test cases into fuzzing seeds. For this goal, a transferred seed must execute on the target DBMS, retain testing-relevant SQL structures, and remain parsable and mutable by the downstream fuzzer.
 
 \textbf{Different challenges.} A fuzzing seed is an ordered sequence of independently executed statements, and later statements may depend on the target-side state produced by earlier statements. For example, in \texttt{ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMN b INT; SELECT b FROM t;}, the \texttt{SELECT} is valid only if the translated \texttt{ALTER TABLE} has produced the expected column on the target DBMS. When the target dialect changes a type, a constraint, or a DDL form, subsequent statements must follow the translated target state rather than the source state. Translating each statement correctly in isolation is therefore insufficient. Our focus is on maintaining this evolving target-side context across an ordered test sequence, beyond translating a self-contained query or preserving an oracle relation. \sedar{} addresses the same setting but translates each statement using a source-side sub-schema and does not maintain translated target-side context across statements.
 
-\textbf{Different methods.} AQT is built on a standard SQL AST; its contribution is not the tree representation itself but the cross-statement information it adds and how this information is consumed during transfer. First, AQT records forward read-after-write dependency edges between statements. These edges identify dependency-relevant preceding statements for context selection during instantiation and repair, while the original statement order is retained. Second, AQT records the cumulative schema context of the source test case, including object-state summaries such as object existence, table definitions, column names and types, constraints, and DDL-induced schema changes. During instantiation, \tool{} maintains the corresponding target-side context and updates it after each translated statement. Each statement is thus instantiated and repaired against the translated target state. Third, abstract labels separate dialect-specific constructs from the surrounding structure, so that dialect-aware instantiation remains local while the original structure is preserved. Finally, \tool{} uses parser and execution feedback to repair transferred seeds before mutation, whereas \sedar{} temporarily comments out parser-incompatible fragments during mutation and restores them afterward. As described in their papers, the workflows of CrackSQL, RISE, and QTRAN do not propagate target-side context across the statements of a test case. We have updated Table~I and Sections~II and~VI to reflect these differences.
+\textbf{Different methods.} AQT is built on a standard SQL AST; its contribution is not the tree representation itself but the cross-statement information it adds and how this information is consumed during transfer. First, AQT records forward read-after-write dependency edges between statements. These edges identify dependency-relevant preceding statements for context selection during instantiation and repair, while the original statement order is retained. Second, AQT records the cumulative schema context of the source test case, including object-state summaries such as object existence, table definitions, column names and types, constraints, and DDL-induced schema changes. During instantiation, \tool{} maintains the corresponding target-side context and updates it after each translated statement. Each statement is thus instantiated and repaired against the translated target state. Third, abstract labels separate dialect-specific constructs from the surrounding structure, so that dialect-aware instantiation remains local while the original structure is preserved. Finally, \tool{} uses parser and execution feedback to repair transferred seeds before mutation, whereas \sedar{} temporarily comments out parser-incompatible fragments during mutation and restores them afterward. As described in their papers, the workflows of CrackSQL, RISE, and QTRAN do not propagate target-side context across the statements of a test case. We have updated Table~I and Sections~II (Background and Motivation) and~VI (Related Work) to reflect these differences.
 
-\textbf{Different results.} The evaluation in Section~IV provides evidence for the practical value of this design. Disabling cross-statement context propagation reduces execution validity from 0.716 to 0.523, supporting the contribution of target-side context propagation. Replacing dependency-based context selection with a recency-based window or the full context reduces execution validity to 0.599 and 0.643, respectively, while increasing input tokens (\commentref{1.2}). At the system level, \tool{} improves execution validity by 130\% and branch coverage by 14\% over \sedar{}, and the controlled mutation-handling comparison is described in \metareviewref{1}.
+\textbf{Different results.} The evaluation in Section~IV (Evaluation) provides evidence for the practical value of this design. Disabling cross-statement context propagation reduces execution validity from 0.716 to 0.523, supporting the contribution of target-side context propagation. Replacing dependency-based context selection with a recency-based window or the full context reduces execution validity to 0.599 and 0.643, respectively, while increasing input tokens (\commentref{1.2}). At the system level, \tool{} improves execution validity by 130\% and branch coverage by 14\% over \sedar{}, and the controlled mutation-handling comparison is described in \metareviewref{1}.
 
 
 \metareviewcomment{3} Clarify how MORPH differs from and complements SmartFuzz in seed construction. The additional comparison is useful but limited in its experimental scope. The revision should acknowledge these limitations and avoid overstating comparative advantages.
 
 \reply
-We have clarified how \tool{} differs from and complements SmartFuzz in Section~VI, and we have extended the comparison in Section~IV. SmartFuzz uses LLMs to synthesize new seeds from features extracted from official DBMS documentation and historical bug-triggering inputs, whereas \tool{} transfers existing test cases from mature DBMSs, which cover a broad range of SQL features, and preserves their cross-statement structures. The two approaches therefore provide complementary seeds: SmartFuzz targets bug-prone features, whereas \tool{} provides broad feature coverage.
