 \reviewcomment{2.2} Regarding the characterization of SEDAR's comment-and-restore mechanism\textemdash{}if both MORPH and SEDAR ultimately feed compatible seeds to GRIFFIN, what is the exact difference in their post-mutation handling? Do seeds repaired by MORPH produce semantically equivalent mutated queries, whereas SEDAR's approach loses that equivalence? If not, what is the claimed advantage?
 
 \reply
-We have clarified the difference between the two mutation-handling strategies in Section~IV. \tool{} repairs parser-incompatible fragments before mutation, so the seeds fed to \griffin{} are already compatible with both the fuzzer parser and the target DBMS, and no post-mutation handling is needed. \sedar{} instead temporarily comments out parser-incompatible fragments during mutation and restores them afterward; restored fragments that contain translation errors therefore remain unrepaired and may cause the mutated test case to fail on the target DBMS, as illustrated by the motivating example in Section~II. We have also stated explicitly that neither strategy guarantees semantic equivalence of the mutated queries. The advantage of \tool{} is therefore not equivalence after mutation, but that it avoids carrying unrepaired translation errors into mutated test cases. Under controlled conditions with the same 300 translated seeds per target DBMS, \tool{}'s handling achieves 4.0\%--27.2\% higher branch coverage than \sedar{}'s handling. Before mutation, \tool{} also achieves higher result and state equivalence than \sedar{} on the sampled MySQL-to-DuckDB cases (52.0\%/74.0\% vs. 38.0\%/69.0\%). Details can be found in \metareviewref{1} and \commentref{1.1}.
